{0 Streaming}

Parseff provides two streaming interfaces for input that arrives incrementally:

- {!module:Parseff.Source}: pull-based streaming (files, channels, sockets,
  sequences, Eio flows).
- {!module:Parseff.Push}: push-based streaming where your caller owns the read
  loop (Lwt, Async, callback APIs).

Ordinary [unit -> 'a] parsers work unchanged with {!val:Parseff.parse},
{!val:Parseff.Source.parse}, and {!val:Parseff.Push.parse}.

{1 Pull streaming: [Source.parse]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.parse} runs a parser pulling bytes from a
{!type:Parseff.Source.t} on demand.

{@ocaml skip[
val Source.parse :
  ?max_depth:int ->
  ?backtrack_window:int ->
  ?diagnostics:Diagnostics.t ->
  ?trace:(trace_event -> unit) ->
  ?consume:[ `All | `Partial ] ->
  Source.t ->
  (unit -> 'a) ->
  ('a, error) Stdlib.result
]}

{@ocaml skip[
let ic = open_in "data.json" in
let source = Parseff.Source.of_channel ic in
let result = Parseff.Source.parse source json in
close_in ic;
result
]}

- [~consume] defaults to [`All], pulling one extra byte after the parser
  returns to check EOF without reading the rest of the source. Pass
  [~consume:`Partial] to leave remaining bytes unread.
- [~backtrack_window] bounds how much buffered history Parseff retains for
  backtracking (default: [65536] bytes; minimum [2]). Exceeding it fails with
  [Backtrack_window_exceeded].
- Error positions are byte offsets from where this parse starts in the stream.
  [found] text covers only whole characters already buffered (Parseff does not
  pull more bytes just to lengthen a snippet), so it can be a prefix of the
  text {!val:Parseff.parse} reports and [range.stop] can be smaller. [Text ""]
  means no whole character is buffered at the failure position (it is at the
  end of the input read so far, or the character there is incomplete): the
  range is empty and the message has no [found] part.
- [~diagnostics] collects diagnostics as in {!val:Parseff.parse}. See
  {!page:diagnostics}.

{1 Streaming multiple values: [Source.parse_each]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.parse_each} parses a lazy sequence of values (such as
NDJSON or log records) from one source, bounding memory by
[~backtrack_window].

{@ocaml skip[
val Source.parse_each :
  ?max_depth:int ->
  ?backtrack_window:int ->
  ?diagnostics:Diagnostics.t ->
  ?trace:(trace_event -> unit) ->
  ?pending:(unit -> bool) ->
  Source.t ->
  (unit -> 'a) ->
  ('a, error) Stdlib.result Seq.t
]}

Each element runs the parser like [Source.parse ~consume:`Partial]. The parser
must consume its trailing separator (a zero-width run yields an [Error]). The
sequence stops at EOF or after the first [Error], and is ephemeral (forcing an
element twice raises [Invalid_argument]). Pass [~pending] if your parser
buffers lookahead tokens past its value: the run after [pending] returns
[true] may consume no input, but a second zero-width run in a row at the same
position yields an [Error]. {!val:Parseff.Push.parse_each} follows the
same rule.

{@ocaml[
let record () =
  let id = Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') in
  let _ = Parseff.char '\n' in
  int_of_string id

let () =
  Parseff.Source.parse_each (Parseff.Source.of_string "1\n22\n333\n") record
  |> Seq.iter (function
       | Ok id -> Printf.printf "%d\n" id
       | Error e -> prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error e))
]}

{1 Source constructors}

The next [Source.parse] or [Source.parse_each] on the same source starts where
the last one stopped (see {!val:Parseff.Source.parse}), so a message head and
its body can be parsed in turn, and a bad record can be skipped. Positions
restart at each parse; {!val:Parseff.Source.offset} gives the absolute byte
offset where the next parse starts.

{2 [Source.of_string]}

Wraps a string as a source (useful for testing streaming code paths).

{@ocaml skip[val of_string : string -> Source.t]}

{2 [Source.of_channel]}

Reads from an [in_channel] with buffer size [~buf_size] (default [4096];
raises [Invalid_argument] if [< 1]).

{@ocaml skip[val of_channel : ?buf_size:int -> in_channel -> Source.t]}

{2 [Source.of_chunks]}

Pulls chunks from a callback returning [Some s] for data (empty strings are
skipped) or [None] for EOF. Chunks may be used without copying and must not be
mutated after the callback returns (relevant only with
[Bytes.unsafe_to_string]).

{@ocaml skip[val of_chunks : (unit -> string option) -> Source.t]}

{@ocaml skip[
let source =
  Parseff.Source.of_chunks (fun () ->
      match Http.Body.read body with
      | `Data chunk -> Some chunk
      | `Eof -> None)
in
Parseff.Source.parse source my_parser
]}

{2 [Source.of_seq]}

Reads chunks from a [string Seq.t] (same chunk-immutability rule as
[of_chunks]).

{@ocaml skip[val of_seq : string Seq.t -> Source.t]}

{@ocaml[
let source =
  Parseff.Source.of_seq (List.to_seq [ "1\n"; "22\n"; "333\n" ])
]}

{2 [Source.of_bigstring_chunks]}

Pulls slices [Some (buf, off, len)] of a bigstring ([Bigstringaf.t],
[Cstruct.buffer], [Unix.read_bigarray]) or [None] for EOF. Copies at most 2040
bytes at a time into Parseff's input buffer; slices must remain unchanged until
the next callback invocation, which may reuse the same buffer.

{@ocaml skip[
type bigstring =
  (char, Bigarray.int8_unsigned_elt, Bigarray.c_layout) Bigarray.Array1.t

val of_bigstring_chunks :
  (unit -> (bigstring * int * int) option) -> Source.t
]}

{@ocaml[
let parse_bigstring buf parser =
  let sent = ref false in
  let source =
    Parseff.Source.of_bigstring_chunks (fun () ->
        if !sent then None
        else (
          sent := true;
          Some (buf, 0, Bigarray.Array1.dim buf)))
  in
  Parseff.Source.parse source parser

let () =
  let text = "key=value" in
  let buf =
    Bigarray.Array1.init Bigarray.char Bigarray.c_layout (String.length text)
      (String.get text)
  in
  let pair () =
    let key = Parseff.take_while (fun c -> c <> '=') in
    let _ = Parseff.char '=' in
    (key, Parseff.take_while (fun _ -> true))
  in
  match parse_bigstring buf pair with
  | Ok pair -> assert (pair = ("key", "value"))
  | Error e -> failwith (Parseff.render_error e)
]}

{2 [Source.of_function]}

Low-level escape hatch matching POSIX [read(2)]: [read buf off len] writes up
to [len] bytes into [buf] at [off] and returns the byte count ([0] for EOF).
Starts with a buffer of [~initial_buffer_size] bytes (default [2040]) and grows
once to [max initial_buffer_size 4096] after a read fills it. Prefer
[of_chunks] or [of_seq] unless wrapping [Unix.read], [Unix.recv], or similar
buffer-filling APIs.

{@ocaml skip[
val of_function :
  ?initial_buffer_size:int -> (bytes -> int -> int -> int) -> Source.t
]}

{@ocaml skip[
let source =
  Parseff.Source.of_function ~initial_buffer_size:512 (fun buf off len ->
      Unix.read fd buf off len)
in
Parseff.Source.parse source my_parser
]}

{1 Push streaming: [Push]}

Use {!module:Parseff.Push} when your event loop pushes chunks into the parser
(Lwt, Async, network callbacks).

{@ocaml skip[
module Push : sig
  type 'a t
  type 'a step =
    | Await
    | Done of ('a * string, error) Stdlib.result

  val parse :
    ?max_depth:int ->
    ?backtrack_window:int ->
    ?diagnostics:Diagnostics.t ->
    ?trace:(trace_event -> unit) ->
    (unit -> 'a) ->
    'a t
  val feed : 'a t -> string -> 'a step
  val finish : 'a t -> ('a * string, error) Stdlib.result
  val cancel : 'a t -> unit

  type each_step =
    | Await
    | Stopped of string
    | Error of error
    | Ended

  val parse_each :
    ?max_depth:int ->
    ?backtrack_window:int ->
    ?diagnostics:Diagnostics.t ->
    ?trace:(trace_event -> unit) ->
    ?pending:(unit -> bool) ->
    on_value:('a -> [ `Continue | `Stop ]) ->
    (unit -> 'a) ->
    (string option -> each_step) * (unit -> unit)
end
]}

{!val:Parseff.Push.parse}, {!val:Parseff.Push.feed}, and
{!val:Parseff.Push.finish} parse a single value and return any
leftover bytes in [Done (Ok (value, leftover))]. End your parser with
{!val:Parseff.end_of_input} if you want to require full consumption. To drop
an unfinished parse, for example when the client disconnects, call
{!val:Parseff.Push.cancel}: it releases the {!module:Parseff.Ref} cells and the
[~diagnostics] collector that the parse holds, without giving it more input.

{!val:Parseff.Push.parse_each} parses a stream of values on a single effect
fiber (the push counterpart of {!val:Parseff.Source.parse_each}). It returns a
[push] function and a [cancel] function. Push [Some chunk] for data and [None]
at EOF:

{@ocaml[
let line () =
  let s = Parseff.take_while (fun c -> c <> '\n') in
  let _ = Parseff.char '\n' in
  s

let push_lines chunks =
  let seen = ref [] in
  let push, _cancel =
    Parseff.Push.parse_each
      ~on_value:(fun s ->
        seen := s :: !seen;
        `Continue)
      line
  in
  let rec go = function
    | [] -> push None
    | chunk :: rest -> (
        match push (Some chunk) with
        | Parseff.Push.Await -> go rest
        | step -> step)
  in
  match go chunks with
  | Parseff.Push.Ended -> Ok (List.rev !seen)
  | Parseff.Push.Error e -> Error e
  | Parseff.Push.Await | Parseff.Push.Stopped _ -> assert false

let () = assert (push_lines [ "ab"; "c\nde\n" ] = Ok [ "abc"; "de" ])
]}

{1 Backtracking and [~backtrack_window]}

Parseff retains buffered bytes from the oldest active rewind point to the
newest read byte. Two things pin earlier bytes:

- Open backtracking frames ([or_], [one_of], [look_ahead], repetition loops).
- Primitives building a single value across chunks ([take], [take_while],
  [recognize]), even after {!val:Parseff.commit} or {!val:Parseff.cut}.

To stay within [~backtrack_window]:

- Dispatch early with {!val:Parseff.peek_char} rather than trying long
  speculative branches.
- Call {!val:Parseff.commit} to release the nearest frame once an alternative
  is chosen, or {!val:Parseff.cut} to release every frame inside the enclosing
  {!val:Parseff.context} once a construct is decided.
- Use {!module:Parseff.Iter} to stream items or large byte payloads past
  [~backtrack_window] (see {{!section-iter}[Iter]}).
- Raise [~backtrack_window] only when the grammar genuinely requires longer
  lookahead.

{@ocaml[
let http_method () =
  Parseff.or_
    (fun () ->
      Parseff.literal "GET ";
      Parseff.commit ();
      `GET)
    (fun () ->
      Parseff.literal "POST ";
      Parseff.commit ();
      `POST)
]}

{1:iter [Iter]}

{!module:Parseff.Iter} (unstable: may change in any minor release) passes each
value or byte piece to a callback [f] instead of returning it. Memory stays
bounded by one element or one piece, so the input can be much larger than
[~backtrack_window].

{@ocaml skip[
module Iter : sig
  val many : ?at_least:int -> (unit -> 'a) -> ('a -> unit) -> unit
  val sep_by :
    ?at_least:int -> (unit -> 'a) -> (unit -> 'b) -> ('a -> unit) -> unit
  val take : int -> (Slice.t -> unit) -> unit
  val take_while :
    ?set:Charset.t -> (char -> bool) -> (Slice.t -> unit) -> unit
end
]}

- {!val:Parseff.Iter.many} and {!val:Parseff.Iter.sep_by} work like
  {!val:Parseff.many} and {!val:Parseff.sep_by}, but give each element to [f]
  and do not build a list.
- {!val:Parseff.Iter.take} and {!val:Parseff.Iter.take_while} work like
  {!val:Parseff.take} and {!val:Parseff.take_while}, but give the bytes to [f]
  as non-empty {!type:Parseff.Slice.t} pieces. A piece borrows the input buffer
  and is valid only during [f]. Call {!val:Parseff.Slice.to_string} to keep it.

{@ocaml[
let sum_ints input =
  let total = ref 0 in
  let r =
    Parseff.parse input (fun () ->
        Parseff.Iter.sep_by Parseff.integer (fun () -> Parseff.char ',')
          (fun n -> total := !total + n))
  in
  Result.map (fun () -> !total) r
]}

All four functions share these rules:

- {b Sealing.} Before each call to [f], they seal every open backtracking
  frame, like {!val:Parseff.cut} outside any {!val:Parseff.context}. The source
  can then drop earlier input. A later failure cannot rewind before a value
  that [f] already received. {!val:Parseff.recognize} and
  {!val:Parseff.Slice.recognize} still keep all of their input.
- {b Callback.} [f] runs outside the parser. Do not call parsers or
  {!val:Parseff.warn} inside [f]. An exception from [f] stops the parse and
  propagates out of the runner.
- {b Frames that cannot be sealed.} A call inside {!val:Parseff.look_ahead},
  {!val:Parseff.peek_with}, {!val:Parseff.not_followed_by}, a
  {!val:Parseff.precedence} lookup, or {!val:Parseff.capture} without
  [~release:true] raises [Invalid_argument].
  Some other frames raise only when [~diagnostics] is active, so test these
  parsers with [~diagnostics].

See {!module:Parseff.Iter} for the full contract.

{{:https://github.com/davesnx/parseff/blob/main/examples/streaming_json_array/main.ml}[examples/streaming_json_array/main.ml]}
parses 20000 array elements through [Iter.sep_by] under
[Source.parse ~backtrack_window:4096], so the input is far larger than the
window.

{2 [Iter] or [parse_each]}

{table
  {tr
    {th Input}
    {th Use}}
  {tr
    {td A top-level stream of values, such as NDJSON or log records.}
    {td {!val:Parseff.Source.parse_each} or {!val:Parseff.Push.parse_each}.}}
  {tr
    {td Repeated items inside a larger structure, such as the elements of a
        JSON array between its brackets.}
    {td {!val:Parseff.Iter.many} or {!val:Parseff.Iter.sep_by} inside one
        {!val:Parseff.Source.parse}.}}
  {tr
    {td A body larger than [~backtrack_window], such as a length-prefixed
        payload.}
    {td {!val:Parseff.Iter.take} or {!val:Parseff.Iter.take_while}.}}}

{1 Concurrency and domains}

- {b Multiple domains}: {!val:Parseff.parse} and {!val:Parseff.Source.parse}
  store active parse state per domain and can run concurrently across OCaml 5
  domains. Do not share one {!type:Parseff.Source.t} or {!type:Parseff.Push.t}
  concurrently across domains.
- {b Cooperative fibers on one domain}: multiple {!val:Parseff.Source.parse}
  calls (e.g., Eio fibers) and {!module:Parseff.Push} parses can interleave on
  one domain, provided only the source's read function suspends (suspending
  inside a parser callback is not supported).
- {b System threads}: OCaml [Thread]s sharing a domain can preempt mid-parser;
  serialize parses on one domain with a [Mutex] or run them on separate domains.
